 @lucastonoli3256  larger chips don’t reduce cost. It’s actually more expensive because of yields. Larger chips are harder to produce so they yield less so they cost significantly more. Because it costs more to keep the yields high and you have to buy more surface area per chip so you get less of them.  Where as smaller chips have a much higher success rate and so they cost less. And they are smaller per wafer so you get more of them. 
It’s like the main reason amd is able to always outprice nvidia and Intel. 
It’s the fundamental reason behind everything they do.

